  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 156 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nBy Friday night the low pressure system discussed in the SHORT\nTERM discussion above will be departing to the east/southeast,\nwith some showers and perhaps a few thunderstorms lingering into\nthe night before likewise departing. By Saturday the\nsouthwestern U.S. deep-layer ridge will have been shunted\nwestward by the trough deepening over the central U.S., being\ncentered somewhere around UT/AZ/NV by Saturday afternoon. This\nwill result in cooler northerly mid-level steering flow over\nIowa. Meanwhile, a surface ridge will build down the Upper\nMidwest in wake of Friday's low pressure system, bringing modest\nnortherly breezes to Iowa by Saturday, then variable winds later\nin the weekend. While Friday temperatures will thus be generally\ncool due to rain and cloud cover, this weekend will present a\nmore enjoyable \"cool\" weather spell with clearing skies,\ngenerally light winds, and highs in the low to mid 80s. This\nwill be a far cry from the intense heat experienced across the\nregion last weekend!\n\nNext Monday will be largely the same as the weekend, with highs\naround the low to mid-80s, mostly sunny skies, and light winds\nas the surface ridge begins to move away to the east late in the\nday. Another surface trough/front will then approach from the\nDakotas on Monday night and Tuesday, then stall out somewhere\nacross the region around the middle of next week. Just after\nthis, a broad 500 MB trough will round the base of a large gyre\nover Canada, passing through the Upper Midwest and providing\nsome modest forcing for ascent sometime between Tuesday night\nand Thursday or so. This is a classic set-up for multiple rounds\nof showers and thunderstorms along the stalled frontal zone,\nhowever, it remains to be seen where that will occur or how the\ntiming and details will shake out. At any rate, after a dry\nperiod from Friday night or Saturday through at least Monday,\nsome chances for rain will return around the middle of next\nweek.",
